WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.150
EMIL KINER: Hi, my
name is Emil Kiner.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.100
I'm the product manager
for Google Cloud

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.125
Armor on the network security
product team at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:13.750
Today, I'll be talking
to you about how

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.970
to protect your applications
from distributed

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.100
denial of service attacks
as well as targeted web

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.010
attacks using Cloud Armor.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.380
We'll start off with an
overview of network security

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.120
in general on Google Cloud.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.130
From there, we'll
discuss all the latest

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.590
and greatest
announcements and new

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.480
features that we've
launched for Cloud Armor

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.250
over the past several months.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.000
After that, we'll dive
in and discuss in depth

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.540
how application protection
and DDoS defense works.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.040
And then we'll
discuss some customer

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:45.190
use cases about how to
protect your applications

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.380
in real-world scenarios.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.660
Security follows a shared
responsibility model

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.010
on Google Cloud.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.320
We work to secure our
network and infrastructure

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.780
and provide customers the
tools necessary to do so

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.440
in their own projects.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.960
We enable and
encourage customers

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.900
to follow a defense-in-depth
approach and deploy security

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.590
controls at various levels of
their stack and infrastructure

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:08.380
to enforce access
controls and ensure data

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:12.580
and mission-critical workloads
remain private and secure.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.440
On Google Cloud, we have several
key network security controls.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.020
Starting from the outside
in, we have Cloud Armor,

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.480
the identity aware proxy,
and the global load

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.490
balancers as our
layer 7 controls

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:26.140
at the edge of the network.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.600
Cloud Armor, in conjunction
with the global LBs,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.480
protect applications
from DDoS attacks.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:34.150
The Identity Aware Proxy is
part of our BeyondCorp line

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.760
of products to allow access
to internal applications

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.980
without needing a VPN.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.740
Firewalls, in red,
apply at the VPC level

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.130
and can be used to customize
access for north-south traffic

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.450
as well as east-west traffic
into, out of, or through VPCs.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.790
Firewalls are used to
define VPC boundaries

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:56.780
but can be as
granular as per host.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.330
VPC Service Controls,
in blue, can

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:03.320
be used to control access
to and from GCP services

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.370
and managed APIs, such as
BigQuery or Cloud Storage

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:13.760
to or from the internet,
VPCs, or on-premise sources.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.500
In yellow, we have
packet mirroring

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:19.370
that allows you to deploy a tap
and mirror packets at the VM

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.340
or container cluster boundary
to a configurable collector

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.570
destination.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.270
Finally, we also support
sending outbound traffic

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.410
through Cloud NAT as well as
various interconnected VPN

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.560
options to enhance
privacy and security.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.690
Cloud load balancing
is a critical component

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.120
of the end-to-end
network security story.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.150
We have three flavors of global
load balancing-- the HTTPS load

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.830
balancer, the SSL proxy,
and the TCP proxy.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.750
The first two load
balancers are SSL

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:54.020
terminating, meaning you
are able to offload the SSL

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.690
termination and decryption
to our edge proxies,

00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:59.420
and we'll handle enforcing
SSL policies upstream

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.290
of your infrastructure.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.990
By uploading your certificates
and configuring custom SSL

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.920
policies, you can ensure
only pre-approved SSL/TLS

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.650
versions and ciphers are
allowed on a per-VIP basis.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.650
All of the global
load balancers employ

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:19.130
a two-tier global anycast
based system rather than

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.340
a DNS-based load
balancing mechanism.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.670
We broadcast the load
bouncer's IP address globally,

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.650
and end user requests
are routed to

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.230
and terminated at the
geographically nearest point

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.600
of presence, or POP, to them.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.110
From there, the request
is coalesced and proxied

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:38.990
over Google's fiber backbone
to the customer backend

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:43.230
in the geographically nearest
region with available capacity.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.170
At each stage of the process,
we optimize on latency,

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.170
taking into account capacity
if the underlying application

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.015
is deployed across
multiple regions.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.690
Cloud Armor is the web
application firewall and DDoS

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.970
mitigation service,
able to provide layer

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.110
7 protection and filtering
for workloads deployed on GCP,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:06.610
on prem, or with other
infrastructure providers.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.710
Cloud Armor is deeply integrated
with the global load balancing

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.590
infrastructure and is
able to inspect and filter

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:17.029
incoming requests after the
SSL termination has occurred.

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:19.640
More specifically,
with Cloud Armor,

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.200
customers are able to protect
their HTTP-fronted applications

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.490
from DDos attack, filter
incoming requests by geography,

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.150
or most L7 parameters, like
request headers or cookies.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.730
Moreover, as a
web-application firewall,

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.740
Cloud Armor contains
pre-configured rules

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.960
to prevent against the
most common attacks

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:41.390
and vulnerability
exploit attempts.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.460
Users have access to
real-time telemetry

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:46.460
in the form of logs sent
to Cloud Logging containing

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.400
Cloud Armor's decisions
on a per-request basis;

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.810
a monitoring
dashboard that gives

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.470
granular views of allowed,
denied, or previewed traffic;

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.570
as well as a
correlated WAF security

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.180
findings that are sent to the
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.940
As of March of this
year, a rich set

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:06.800
of WAF capabilities
for Cloud Armor

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.270
was made generally available.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.450
Preconfigured rules
can help mitigate

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.330
the OWASP top 10 risks.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:15.680
We have ported over the
mod security core rule set,

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.260
introducing rules
to detect and block

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.890
SQL injection and cross-site
scripting attempts as generally

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:22.910
available.

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:26.450
Additionally, rules for local
file inclusion, remote file

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:28.820
inclusion, and
remote code execution

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.710
are available as a beta.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.440
The rest of the core rule
set is targeted for release

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.020
over the course of this year.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:39.260
With geo-based rules, customers
can enforce access controls

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.290
based on the source
geography of each request.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.410
The IP-to-geo mappings are
sourced from Google's own geo

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:50.040
team, so you can be
sure of its accuracy.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.340
We've also launched an
extensible rules language

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.280
that allows users to configure
custom layer 7 filtering

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.920
policies across request
headers, request

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.170
parameters, and cookies.

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.140
Finally, Cloud Armor is now
integrated with the Security

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.480
Command Center with
Cloud Armor findings

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.780
as well as assets being
sent to the CSCC dashboard

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.300
to alert defenders
of potential attacks

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.200
against protected
applications and websites.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.470
We have recently
greatly increased

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.600
the flexibility of
Cloud Armor deployments

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.440
to protect an expanded set
of customer infrastructure

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.770
on GCP as well as in hybrid
use cases located on prem

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:30.680
or in other cloud providers.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.440
Cloud Armor now supports
protecting cloud CDM origin

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.740
servers by enforcing security
policies on dynamic requests,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:42.020
as well as cache misses destined
for the CDM origin server.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.240
Customer applications
are often complex,

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.030
serving both cacheable
static content as well as

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:49.850
dynamic requests from
the same services.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.090
With Cloud Armor able to
inspect and filter requests

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:55.380
destined to CDN origin servers.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.310
Users can now mitigate
computationally expensive cache

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:01.040
busting attacks, as
well as help protect

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.380
dynamic portions of
websites and applications

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:06.490
from OWASP's top 10 risks.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:09.340
Enterprises often need to
enforce a consistent set

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.020
of security controls
for their applications,

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.410
no matter where they are
deployed, whether they

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:17.680
are migrating to GCP or
running in permanently hybrid

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:19.480
configurations.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.840
We recently launched support
for internet network and point

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:26.080
groups that allows customers to
leverage all of Google's Edge

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.140
infrastructure, including
cloud load balancers, Cloud

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:32.810
CDM, and Cloud Armor to protect
their website or application,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.000
no matter where they are hosted.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.220
Now Google can help
protect year applications,

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.190
whether from DDoS attacks
or other common web

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:43.490
attacks without having to
deploy the application directly

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:45.540
on GCP.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.810
Finally, GKE ingress support
for Cloud Armor has recently

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:50.610
reached general availability.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:53.610
GKE users can help protect
their containerized workloads

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.550
by placing them behind
the cloud load balancers

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:59.490
and configuring the Cloud Armor
security policy for layer 7

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:00.920
filtering and WAF use cases.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.180
Also we are proud to
announce the availability

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.760
as a beta, a highly
requested capability

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.550
to allow or deny traffic
through a Cloud Armor security

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:17.470
policy based on a
pre-configured, named IP list.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.830
Many of our customers receive
traffic into their GCP projects

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:24.640
from upstream service
providers, such as other CDMs.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.410
Prior to this
capability, customers

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.540
would have to self-manage
the list of IPs

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.277
from upstream
providers to ensure

00:08:30.277 --> 00:08:32.860
they can receive those requests
through a Cloud Armor security

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:34.220
policy.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.460
Now customers can
configure a security policy

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.000
to deny all traffic from
the internet by default

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.120
and allow only traffic
from desired IP ranges

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.090
by referencing the
named IP lists.

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:48.970
For now, the named IP
lists are global in nature

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:50.800
and not user defined.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:53.230
We are launching the beta
with three launch partners--

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:55.650
Fastly, Cloudflare,
and Imperva--

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:58.150
but intend to add the other
service providers over time.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.830
Finally, we are excited
and proud to announce

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.340
the beta launch of Cloud
Armor Managed Protection,

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:10.430
a set of DDoS mitigation
and WAF services

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:14.000
offered at two service
tiers, standard and plus,

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:16.580
which bundles together all of
the features and capabilities

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:19.400
of Cloud Armor with additional
value-added services,

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.830
all for an enterprise
friendly, predictable

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.480
monthly subscription.

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.940
Last month, we published
a blog post announcing

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:29.000
that Google had successfully
detected and mitigated

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.800
the largest DDoS attack
ever recorded to date.

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:35.650
Now, with managed protection
Google Cloud customers

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:37.900
can leverage the same
Edge capacity as well as

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:41.140
DDoS defense expertise to
protect their own applications

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.390
and other publicly
exposed workloads,

00:09:43.390 --> 00:09:45.910
with a subscription plan
that offers enterprise

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:47.920
friendly and
critically predictable

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.270
pricing, which is not driven
by incoming request volumes.

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:56.670
Cloud Armor WAF usage
includes rules, policies,

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:59.280
and requests are included
in the subscription plan,

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:01.640
meaning Cloud Armor monthly
price will remain relatively

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:05.420
fixed, even if you are targeted
by a high-volume layer 7 DDoS

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.500
attack that needs to be
mitigated by Cloud Armor.

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.390
Over time, we will
expand the services

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:13.400
offered in the
plus tier, starting

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.190
with the upcoming
Google-curated rule sets,

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:19.448
like the named IP lists
announced previously.

00:10:19.448 --> 00:10:21.240
For more information
on managed protection,

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.440
please refer to the Cloud
Armor blog announcing the beta,

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.510
as well as the product
page for a sign-up form

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:28.062
to get more information.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.760
On GCP, customers enjoy
an unrivaled level

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:39.030
of DDoS protection from a
wide variety of attack types.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.820
It is important to understand
that DDoS protection cloud

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.130
customers are able to
receive is the same that

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:45.953
has been developed
over the past 20 years

00:10:45.953 --> 00:10:47.370
to protect and
ensure availability

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.090
of Google's own services.

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.160
Thanks to the global
nature of Google's network,

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:54.650
we are able to absorb and
dissipate or otherwise

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:58.520
mitigate layer 3 and layer 4
network or volumetric attacks

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.580
across various components
in our global load balancing

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:03.050
infrastructure.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.240
The way this automatic
mitigation works

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:07.520
is that all three types
of global load balancers

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.580
will only proxy requests
back to the customer backend

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:15.080
service when that request
has completed a three-way TCP

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:16.500
handshake.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.260
For volumetric and
protocol-based DDoS attacks,

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.260
like UDP amplification
or reflection, as well as

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:27.340
TCP floods, the TCP handshake
is actually never established,

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.180
so we can safely drop this
unwelcome traffic far upstream

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:31.900
of your infrastructure.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.180
This happens
automatically upstream

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.140
of customer infrastructure.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:38.130
Next, Cloud Armor
security policies

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:40.500
can be configured and
attached to the load balanced

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.650
backend services
to further provide

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:47.760
layer 7 application layer
protection and access controls.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:49.260
Cloud Armor security
policies can

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:50.970
be configured to
limit access based

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:54.750
on source IP or geo, a
pre-configured WAF rule set,

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.090
as well as our fully
customizable rules language

00:11:57.090 --> 00:11:59.895
to craft custom layer
7 filtering policies.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:06.200
Diving in a bit further,
Cloud Armor security policies

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.580
can simultaneously invoke
pre-configured WAF rules,

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:13.400
as well as user-defined rules
to inspect request headers,

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:15.140
parameters, and cookies.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:18.650
Policies are stored,
evaluated, and enforced

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.710
at the edge of Google's network
far upstream of customer's

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.330
infrastructure.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.280
In the example on this
slide, we have a policy

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.210
that first denies access to
any external clients requesting

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:33.400
access to the admin portal
of our example application.

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.070
Next, the Cloud
Armor security policy

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.760
is configured to invoke
preconfigured WAF rules

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.210
to detect and block known
signatures for SQL injection

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.950
and cross-site
scripting attacks,

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:45.260
which collectively have
dozens of subsignatures

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:47.010
under the hood.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:49.340
Finally, if the
request is neither

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.560
targeting the admin
portal nor containing

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:54.350
a SQL injection or cross-site
scripting signature,

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.390
we will allow that
traffic as configured

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:57.440
through the default rule.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:04.610
Now, we'll discuss some example
use cases for how customers

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:08.210
can protect their applications.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.710
In order to protect
your application

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:12.620
from volumetric and
protocol-based DDoS attacks,

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.770
all that is necessary is to
deploy one of the global load

00:13:15.770 --> 00:13:19.610
balancers in front of
your HTTP or TCP workload.

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:22.250
Once fronted by the load
balancers, Cloud Armor,

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.620
in conjunction with the
LDs, will automatically

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.100
mitigate DDoS attacks like
DNS amplification attacks,

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.210
SYN floods, and other common
layer 3, layer 4 DDoS attacks.

00:13:32.210 --> 00:13:35.240
The only requests that will be
proxy back to your applications

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:38.480
are ones that have completed
the three-way handshake

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.185
and are well-formed
layer 7 requests.

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:46.065
To add layer 7 protection
for your applications,

00:13:46.065 --> 00:13:48.440
the first thing you need to
do is configure a Cloud Armor

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.110
security policy and attach it
to the backend service that

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:54.850
is hosting the application
or workload to be protected.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:57.700
Cloud Armor security policies
are used to customize access

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:59.650
to protected resources.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:02.050
There is a one-to-many
relationship between policies

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:04.720
and backends, meaning
one policy can

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:06.970
be attached to multiple
backend services,

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:09.880
but each backend can only have
one security policy protecting

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:11.050
it.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:14.290
Each policy consists of a
prioritized list of rules

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.000
as well as a default rule.

00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:18.640
As an incoming request tries
to reach the customer backend

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:21.460
service, Cloud Armor
will evaluate each rule

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.230
in priority order
against the traffic.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:26.380
The first matching
rule will win,

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:29.260
meaning it will dictate
the action to take--

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.580
allow or deny in this case.

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.520
If the traffic doesn't
match any rules,

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:35.560
then the action configured
with the default rule

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:38.470
will be applied to
either allow or deny

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.680
the traffic through to
the targeted backend.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:47.230
Visibility and
telemetry is key for

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.140
any comprehensive application
protection solution.

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:51.880
Cloud Armor exposes
this information

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.550
in near real time across
several telemetry platforms

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:56.270
at Google Cloud.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:58.560
Per-request logs are
captured and sent

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.650
to Cloud Logging
containing all decisions

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:03.870
that Cloud Armor makes with
regard to layer 7 requests,

00:15:03.870 --> 00:15:06.710
as well as which
rules fired and why.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:08.900
Real-time telemetry
for request volumes

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.480
is available at various
levels of granularity

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:15.167
through cloud monitoring in
our preconfigured dashboard

00:15:15.167 --> 00:15:17.000
to help you visualize
and build the learning

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.440
policies against changes
in traffic patterns.

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:23.570
Finally, correlated
security findings

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:25.460
about unexpected
traffic spikes are

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:27.440
sent to the Security
Command Center

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:29.690
to trigger investigation
and possibly

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.730
incident response workflows.

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:35.890
Putting it all together,
here is an example

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.830
of a sophisticated use case
that leverages the ability

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.990
to dynamically update
Cloud Armor security

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:45.160
policies through our feature
rich REST API or CLI.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.800
Here, we see an application
fronted by a cloud load

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:51.190
balancer with Google
Cloud Armor enabled.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:54.220
Telemetry is collected until
monitoring and analytics

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.800
workflow, either
homegrown by the customer,

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.860
commercial off-the-shelf,
or using GCP's native data

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.830
analytics tools,
such as BigQuery.

00:16:02.830 --> 00:16:05.410
Other sources of
telemetry and logging

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:08.710
are added to the system,
including from the application,

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:12.070
from Cloud Armor, and other
devices on the network.

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:14.680
Next, threat for fraud
detection algorithms

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:16.960
will correlate the
various telemetry

00:16:16.960 --> 00:16:20.140
and generate additional
signatures of malicious traffic

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:22.500
to block upstream.

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:25.530
These signatures can then
be dynamically pushed

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:28.380
into the Cloud Armor
security policy as a new rule

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.710
to quickly block the newly
detected malicious behavior,

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:35.720
all at the edge of
Google's network.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:38.700
With that, I wanted to thank
you for joining me today.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.570
If you have any other
questions, please check out

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.060
our recently published
blogs or the product page.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.630
And don't hesitate to reach out
with any detailed questions,

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:48.810
and we'd be happy to
follow up with you.

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:50.470
Thank you.

